/// The platform's module: answers, to `build`, the platform the
/// host hands every program, whose members are the program's standard
/// output, the collections, the kernel classes a program may name and a
/// stopwatch. The host instantiates it once, at boot.
///
/// theMirror: the VM mirror, which the host alone holds.
/// kernel: the kernel module's instance, whose classes `KernelNamespace`
///     hands out.
///
/// The module behind the platform a module is handed (§8.3): a shipped
/// core-library module, booted by the host with the VM mirror and the
/// kernel instance (`docs/design/core-library.md`, "The module table and
/// the boot").
///
/// The factory pattern: this root holds what is privileged -- the mirror,
/// and the kernel's classes `KernelNamespace` hands out, never the kernel
/// instance -- and the host never hands its instance to anyone. It
/// instantiates the root once, sends it `build`, its one public
/// member, and hands every program that platform, an instance of the
/// nested class `Platform`, whose factory is a harmless `new`. What a
/// program receives are instances of the nested classes, the facets,
/// through the platform's members; each reaches the mirror and the kernel's
/// classes lexically, as a member of this root, never through a slot of its
/// own, so every one of them writes through the one mirror. The nested
/// classes are private, so even
/// a holder of this root's instance could send it nothing but
/// `build`: a second line of defence behind the instance being
/// unreachable. The collections module is another such root
/// (`CollectionsFactory.ns`), whose one instance the host builds at boot
/// and this root reaches through the mirror's `moduleInstanceNamed:`,
/// keeping it in a private lazy slot here; the platform hands out only the
/// facet it answers. Nothing a program can name answers this root's
/// instance or its class, so no program can build a second one over
/// something that is not the mirror. Keep it so: no code whose receiver is
/// this root's instance may send it anything it does not understand, since
/// a `MessageNotUnderstood` hands its receiver to whoever catches it
/// (`docs/design/core-library.md`, "No program reaches a shipped module's
/// root").
///
/// The factory's parameters are copied into slots because neither a lazy
/// slot nor a method may name them (§6); of the kernel only the classes
/// `KernelNamespace` hands out are kept, not the kernel instance itself. A send to `vmMirror` here is a
/// mirror send, as in `Kernel.ns`: this module is resolved with
/// `resolve_kernel`, which only shipped source is.
///
/// Everything here but `Stopwatch` is a value (§3.1.1): stateless but for
/// what the host hands the root and the collections module the boot built,
/// which the root reaches through the mirror. A `Stopwatch` keeps its
/// reading in slots, so it is an object, as `CollectionsFactory.ns`'s
/// `List` is.
class PlatformFactory usingVmMirror: theMirror kernel: kernel = Object <: Value (
  |
    /// The VM mirror the factory was given: the one object through which
    /// a member here reaches a native operation. Never handed out.
    private vmMirror = theMirror.
    /// The kernel's `Exception`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Exception = kernel Exception.
    /// The kernel's `Error`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Error = kernel Error.
    /// The kernel's `MessageNotUnderstood`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private MessageNotUnderstood = kernel MessageNotUnderstood.
    /// The kernel's `Halt`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Halt = kernel Halt.
    /// The kernel's `Message`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Message = kernel Message.
    /// The kernel's `ArgumentError`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private ArgumentError = kernel ArgumentError.
    /// The kernel's `CannotReturn`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private CannotReturn = kernel CannotReturn.
    /// The kernel's `CannotInstantiate`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private CannotInstantiate = kernel CannotInstantiate.
    /// The kernel's `Closure`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Closure = kernel Closure.
    /// The kernel's `Future`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Future = kernel Future.
    /// The kernel's `Proxy`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Proxy = kernel Proxy.
    /// The kernel's `Collection`, which `KernelNamespace` hands out.
    private Collection = kernel Collection.
  |
) (
  /// The one collections module, the instance the boot built over the
  /// kernel, and the same object on every use.
  ///
  /// The host builds every shipped module once, at boot, and the mirror
  /// answers that instance, so every platform's facet comes from one
  /// factory and hands out one `List` class. Its instance is as private
  /// as this one: only the facets its `build` answers leave this module.
  lazy private collectionsModule = vmMirror moduleInstanceNamed: #CollectionsFactory ifFail: [nil].

  /// The platform a program is handed: the namespace of what it may use
  /// beyond the kernel, each member built on first use.
  ///
  /// §8.3 describes the platform as an object that "provides accessors to
  /// various modules"; each is a lazy slot (§6.3.3), built on first use,
  /// so a program pays only for what it asks for. It hands out none of
  /// this module's classes: a program holds the instances its members
  /// answer, and since `class` is protected it reaches no class of
  /// theirs, so it can make no second platform or transcript. `Time`
  /// hands out `Stopwatch`, which a program instantiates itself, and the
  /// collections facet the collections module's classes.
  private class Platform = Value new () (
    /// The program's standard output, a `Transcript`, written as UTF-8.
    /// Built on first use; every later send answers the same one.
    lazy public transcript = Transcript new.

    /// The namespace of the collections module's classes -- `List`,
    /// `Map`, `IdentityMap`, `Set` and the Errors `NotFound` and
    /// `ConcurrentModification` -- and of the kernel's `Collection`.
    /// Built on first use; every later send answers the same one.
    lazy public collections = collectionsModule build.

    /// The namespace of the kernel classes a program may name, a
    /// `KernelNamespace`. Built on first use; every later send answers
    /// the same one.
    lazy public kernel = KernelNamespace new.

    /// The namespace of `Stopwatch`, a `Time`. Built on first use;
    /// every later send answers the same one.
    lazy public time = Time new.
  )

  /// The program's standard output: writes text, line breaks and the
  /// printed form of any object, as UTF-8, in the order they are sent.
  ///
  /// `show:` writes a string as it is and refuses anything else, as an
  /// ordinary error; `print:` writes any object's `printString`, as
  /// canonical Newspeak's transcript does. The leaf never declines, so
  /// the failure blocks never run.
  private class Transcript = Value new () (
    /// Writes `text` to standard output as it is, flushed at once, and
    /// answers the receiver.
    ///
    /// text: a String or a Symbol.
    ///
    /// Signals an ArgumentError carrying `text`, 'show: requires a
    /// String, got …', for any other argument; nothing is written.
    public show: text = (
      ^vmMirror transcript: self show: text ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// Writes a line break to standard output and answers the
    /// receiver.
    public newLine = (
      ^vmMirror transcriptNewLine: self ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// Writes the `printString` of `object` to standard output, as
    /// `show:` does, and answers the receiver.
    ///
    /// object: any object; sent `printString`, whose answer must be a
    /// String or a Symbol, or `show:` signals its ArgumentError.
    public print: object = (
      ^self show: object printString.
    )
  )

  /// `platform kernel`: the kernel classes a program may name that
  /// `Object`'s class accessors do not answer, and `Closure`, which they
  /// answer too.
  ///
  /// The facet over the kernel module that Primordial Soup's `Kernel
  /// wrapping:` answers.
  private class KernelNamespace = Value new () (
    /// Answers the kernel's class `Exception`.
    public Exception = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Exception.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Error`.
    public Error = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Error.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `MessageNotUnderstood`.
    public MessageNotUnderstood = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory MessageNotUnderstood.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Halt`.
    public Halt = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Halt.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Message`.
    public Message = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Message.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `ArgumentError`.
    public ArgumentError = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory ArgumentError.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `CannotReturn`.
    public CannotReturn = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory CannotReturn.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `CannotInstantiate`.
    public CannotInstantiate = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory CannotInstantiate.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Closure`.
    public Closure = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Closure.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Future`.
    public Future = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Future.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's class `Proxy`.
    public Proxy = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Proxy.
    )

    /// Answers the kernel's `Collection` class, the abstract superclass
    /// of `Array`, `List`, `Map` and `Set`, for a program to subclass.
    public Collection = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Collection.
    )
  )

  /// `platform time`: the namespace of the clock's classes, today only
  /// `Stopwatch`.
  ///
  /// Primordial Soup's `Time` facet, holding only what
  /// `benchmarks/are-we-fast-yet/AreWeFastYet.ns` measures with.
  private class Time = Value new () (
    /// Answers this module's class `Stopwatch`: `Stopwatch new`
    /// answers a stopped one reading 0.
    public Stopwatch = (
      ^outer PlatformFactory Stopwatch.
    )
  )

  /// A stopwatch over a monotonic clock in nanoseconds: made stopped,
  /// reading 0, it runs from `start` on. An object, not a value: its
  /// reading changes.
  ///
  /// Primordial Soup's `Stopwatch` (`Time.ns`), narrowed to what
  /// `benchmarks/are-we-fast-yet/AreWeFastYet.ns` sends. It keeps its reading in slots, so it is
  /// an object, not a value. Its readings are nanoseconds since an
  /// origin taken once per process, not the operating system's per-boot
  /// monotonic clock Primordial Soup's primitive reads, so a started
  /// stopwatch loaded into a fresh process reads a start taken against
  /// another origin: its elapsed time there is wrong, and negative
  /// whenever the loading process is younger than the start was.
  private class Stopwatch = Object new (
    |
      /// The nanoseconds of the stopwatch's earlier runs: 0, since
      /// nothing here stops a stopwatch.
      private cumulativeNanoseconds ::= 0.
      /// The clock's reading at `start`, an Integer; nil until
      /// `start`.
      private startNanoseconds ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Answers the nanoseconds that have passed since `start`, an
    /// Integer, read anew on every send; 0 before `start`.
    public elapsedNanoseconds = (
      startNanoseconds isNil ifTrue: [^cumulativeNanoseconds].
      ^cumulativeNanoseconds + (currentMonotonicNanoseconds - startNanoseconds).
    )

    /// Starts the stopwatch and answers it; a running stopwatch keeps
    /// its start.
    public start = (
      startNanoseconds isNil ifFalse: [^self].
      startNanoseconds:: currentMonotonicNanoseconds.
    )

    /// Answers the clock's reading, an Integer: the nanoseconds since
    /// an origin taken once per process, never less than an earlier
    /// reading in the same process.
    private currentMonotonicNanoseconds = (
      ^vmMirror clockMonotonicNanoseconds: self ifFail: [nil].
    )
  )

  /// Answers a new `Platform`: what the host hands every program. The
  /// one public member of the module, named `build` like every shipped
  /// factory's.
  public build = (
    ^Platform new.
  )
)
